@ulidtko Using libsystemd.so.0 is barely better. At least it might not suffer as much from ABI breakage (although it could still do, because we are not letting the compiler check this for us).
But it will sill mean a SOVERSION bump might go unnoticed, and the functionality silently disabled. Also this causes pain on distributions, as transitions to new SOVERSIONs require not just a mass rebuild, but in addition hunting down all theses cases and patching the sources. I think this type of dynamic loading of external shared libraries is in most if not all cases just wrong.

I recommended the plugin thing, because if you want to preserve this kind of run-time optional dependency, then using dlopen() on a plugin under your control is one of the few proper ways to do it. Another option could be to use a helper program instead of a plugin, which could be called only if it's present. So that a distribution that cares can disable its building or split its installation into say another package, to not require libsystemd being pulled by the main process. Keeping the old implementation of the notification support is of course another perfectly valid option. :)

@jtru the intended [GitHub workflows][•] offer ~three alternatives:
 1. Me simply recreating another PR;
 2. You add me as collaborator to your fork, and I push commits to `jtru/better-systemd-integration-v2`;
 3. Me sending a PR *to your fork/branch* and you accepting it.

Both 2 and 3 will result in changes appearing *here*, in this PR to antirez/redis.

If you want a little practice with this -- let's exercise 3, just to see how it works :) I'll send a PR to your fork in a minute.

[•]: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/22237609/adding-to-someone-else-s-pull-request-on-a-github-project-that-i-do-not-own

